## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that while President Trump's executive order on September 24, 2020, was issued and claimed to affirm protections for preexisting conditions, it was widely reported as "largely symbolic" and not a substantive legal change. The evidence explicitly shows that major news outlets, including *The Washington Post*, *The New York Times*, NBC News, and wire services, covered the executive order and its symbolic nature. The White House also held a press briefing with senior officials to discuss it.

Therefore, based on the recorded evidence, the claim that "no news coverage was given" is directly refuted. The evidence demonstrates that significant media coverage did occur.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The executive order signed by President Trump on September 24, 2020, was widely reported by major news outlets, including [*The New York Times*](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/trump-signs-eos-health-care-does-little-change-existing-legislation-n1241022), [*The Washington Post*](https://www.salon.com/2020/09/25/insult-to-every-family-trump-unveils-bogus-health-care-plan-that-doesnt-actually-do-anything/), [NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/trump-signs-eos-health-care-does-little-change-existing-legislation-n1241022), and wire services. Coverage consistently described the order as "[largely symbolic](https://kffhealthnews.org/morning-breakout/trump-affirms-preexisting-condition-protections-enacted-by-obamacare/)," characterizing it as a reaffirmation of protections already established under the Affordable Care Act rather than a substantive legal change, with the White House also holding a [press briefing with senior officials](https://www.ajmc.com/view/trump-aims-at-preexisting-conditions-surprise-billing-in-executive-orders-but-details-are-sparse) to discuss the order. Therefore, the claim that "no news coverage was given" is directly and thoroughly refuted by the documented evidence of substantial media attention.